From 403b4f2e32522a7c956383b4a199c1893fc306df Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 20:02:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Fix Path/str confusion left over from the pathlib migration

Single-animal TensorFlow `analyze_videos` fails outright on every platform:

    cv2.error: OpenCV(4.9.0) :-1: error: (-5:Bad argument) in function 'VideoCapture'
    > Overload resolution failed:
    >  - Can't convert object of type 'PosixPath' to 'str' for 'filename'

`collect_video_paths()` returns `list[Path]`, and `compat.analyze_videos`
additionally coerces every user-supplied video with `_coerce_video_paths`
before dispatching, but `AnalyzeVideo` hands that value straight to
`cv2.VideoCapture`, which only accepts `str`. Every other cv2 call site in
the tree already casts -- `auxfun_videos.py`, `pseudo_label.py`,
`pose_estimation_pytorch/data/image.py` -- so this one was simply missed.

That crash is one instance of a class of defect: values that became `Path`
in the pathlib migration now reach APIs, dict keys and string methods that
still expect `str` (and, in one case, the reverse). Upstream's own sweep --
f6b0effa15c3 "Harden path-handling after pathlib migration (#3421)", which
shipped in 3.0.1 -- missed all of the following. Every one of them except
the `extract_frames` filter is still unfixed on the upstream `dev` branch;
see the note on that one below.

* pose_estimation_tensorflow/predict_videos.py, `AnalyzeVideo`

  `cv2.VideoCapture(video)`. Hard crash; single-animal TensorFlow video
  analysis is unusable. The multi-animal branch is unaffected because
  `AnalyzeMultiAnimalVideo` normalises with `Path(video)` and then calls
  `VideoWriter(str(video))`.

* refine_training_dataset/outlier_frames.py, `ExtractFramesbasedonPreselection`

  `cfg["video_sets"].get(video)` with a `Path`. `video_sets` is keyed by
  `str`: `create_project/new.py` writes `os.fspath(...)` and `ProjectConfig`
  types it `dict[str, Any]`. The lookup therefore silently returns `None`
  and the per-video crop is dropped, so outlier frames are extracted
  uncropped and labels are plotted at un-offset coordinates. Silently wrong
  output rather than a crash, and only when `cropping: true`.
  `_read_video_specific_cropping_margins`, in the same file, already does
  the same lookup correctly with `str(video_path)`.

* refine_training_dataset/outlier_frames.py, `find_outliers_in_raw_data`

  `pickle_file.endswith(...)` on a parameter annotated `str | Path`, so the
  documented `Path` half of the annotation raises `AttributeError`.

* utils/conversioncode.py, `analyze_videos_converth5_to_csv` and
  `analyze_videos_converth5_to_nwb`

  The `listofvideos=True` branch passes the caller's list through unchanged
  while `_convert_h5_files_to` was migrated to `Path` and now uses
  `video.name` and `video.stem`, so a list of `str` -- exactly what the
  docstring describes -- raises `AttributeError`. This is the mirror image
  of the same confusion.

* pose_estimation_tensorflow/modelzoo/api/superanimal_inference.py,
  `_video_inference_superanimal`

  `video.split(".")[-1]` on an element of `videos`, which
  `modelzoo/video_inference.py` never coerces, so a `Path` raises
  `AttributeError`. `Path.suffix` is also more correct than splitting on
  the last dot in the whole path.

* utils/plotting.py, `plot_trajectories`

  `",".join(failures)` where `failures` collects elements of the `Path`
  list returned by `collect_video_paths`. `TypeError` instead of the
  diagnostic the branch exists to print, on the very common "you have not
  run `analyze_videos` yet / wrong scorer / wrong track_method" path, and
  after all the per-video work is already done.

* generate_training_dataset/frame_extraction.py, `extract_frames`

  `[v for v in cfg["video_sets"] if v in videos_list]` compares `str`
  config keys against the caller's `videos_list`. The GUI stores selected
  videos as `set[Path]` (`gui/window.py`, `self.files: set[Path]`) and
  passes that set straight through, so nothing ever matches, the extraction
  loop never runs, and `all([])` is `True` -- the user is told "Frame
  extraction failed. Video files must be corrupted." about perfectly good
  videos. Silently wrong, and it is the GUI's normal Extract Frames path.
  `create_project/add.py` shows the intended type: it passes
  `[os.fspath(video) for video in videos]`. The same comparison in the
  `mode="match"` branch is corrected too.

  This is the one item here that upstream has since fixed, in #3449 on
  `dev` (after 3.0.1). That fix is not backported verbatim because it
  arrives as a 202/-129 line rewrite of the module that also introduces a
  new `ValueError` on an unmatched selection; what is taken from it is its
  normalisation, comparing `Path(video)` on both sides, so the behaviour
  here matches upstream's `_filter_config_videos` without the rewrite or
  the new exception.

* gui/tabs/create_videos.py

  `", ".join(failed_videos)` over the same `set[Path]`; `TypeError` in the
  handler, which also skips the `plot_trajectories` call that follows it.
  Reached simply by running the tab twice on the same video, since
  `proc_video` returns `None` when the labeled video already exists.

Each fix normalises at the boundary that carries the requirement, matching
what the surrounding code already does, instead of changing what
`collect_video_paths` returns or sprinkling casts through the callers. In
particular the TensorFlow branch is explicitly excluded from the pathlib
migration upstream (see the note on `_normalize_image_path` in
pose_estimation_tensorflow/datasets/utils.py), so casting to `str` at the
point of use is the idiom it already follows.

Verified by running DeepLabCut's own
examples/testscript_tensorflow_single_animal.py (project creation, frame
extraction, training, evaluation, `analyze_videos` with and without dynamic
cropping and with direct cropping, `create_labeled_video`,
`plot_trajectories`, `extract_outlier_frames`, `merge_datasets`,
`analyzeskeleton`, `filterpredictions`) and
examples/testscript_tensorflow_multi_animal.py end to end, plus the
project's pytest suite.
